  "evaluated_on": "2026-10-15",
  "hypothesis": "enhancing",
  "design": "Difference-in-differences with one treated state: WA general retail (supermarkets, clothing and department stores) against comparison states, net of the same comparison for 472 groups of unaffected WA industries.",
  "comparison": "NSW, Victoria, Queensland, Tasmania, ACT and NT (SA excluded: Adelaide trading change in 2012)",
  "window": "2007-08 to 2017-18 (before COVID)",
  "verdict": "inconclusive",
  "finding_label": "No detectable effect",
  "grade": "Moderate",
  "grade_reason": "One treated state and three pre-reform years, so parallel trends cannot be checked well. The plan's diagnostics pass, and every robustness variant stays inside the main interval.",
  "headline": "Perth's move to 9pm weeknight trading (November 2010) and Sunday trading (August 2012) had no detectable effect on how many general retail businesses started or closed. Entry changed by +0.8 per 100 businesses a year (90% interval −2.4 to +3.7), exit by −0.1 (−2.4 to +2.5).",
  "category_summaries": {
    "competition": "No detectable effect on business entry or exit. Large effects are ruled out if WA retail would otherwise have tracked other states.",
    "prices": "No effect identified. Perth's food prices fell relative to other capitals, but they were already falling before the reform.",
    "productivity": "Not measurable with public data. The spread across comparison states dwarfs any plausible effect, so these are shown for description only."
  },
